{
  "id": "18844_akkasuru",
  "headword": "{悪化|あっか}する",
  "reading": "あっかする",
  "romaji": "akkasuru",
  "part_of_speech": "verb",
  "gloss": "to worsen; to deteriorate",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "to worsen; to deteriorate; to get worse",
      "explanation": "For a condition, situation, or relationship to become worse over time."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "18844_akkasuru_ex1",
      "japanese": "{症状|しょうじょう}が{悪化|あっか}した。",
      "english": "The symptoms worsened.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "18844_akkasuru_ex2",
      "japanese": "{天候|てんこう}が{悪化|あっか}して、{試合|しあい}が{中止|ちゅうし}になった。",
      "english": "The weather deteriorated and the game was canceled.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "18844_akkasuru_ex3",
      "japanese": "{治療|ちりょう}をしないと、{病状|びょうじょう}がさらに{悪化|あっか}する{恐|おそ}れがある。",
      "english": "Without treatment, there is a risk that the condition will deteriorate further.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    }
  ],
  "notes": "An intransitive verb — the subject worsens on its own. To say someone made something worse (transitive), use {悪化|あっか}させる. Commonly used with health ({症状|しょうじょう}が{悪化|あっか}する), weather ({天気|てんき}が{悪化|あっか}する), relationships ({関係|かんけい}が{悪化|あっか}する), and economic conditions ({景気|けいき}が{悪化|あっか}する). The antonym is {改善|かいぜん}する (to improve).",
